4x Physical LEDs (D2 to D5)
Positional Binary Number Representation
Each LED physically maps to a power-of-two positional weight: Bit 0 (\(2^0=1\)), Bit 1 (\(2^1=2\)), Bit 2 (\(2^2=4\)), and Bit 3 (\(2^3=8\)). Together, they visualize any nibble from 0b0000 (0) to 0b1111 (15).


220Ω Current-Limiting Resistors
Output Stage Protection & Ohm’s Law
Protects the STM32 GPIO output push-pull stage from excessive current sinking or sourcing, maintaining the voltage level safely within the 3.3V logic threshold.


Common Ground (GND)
Electrical Reference Potential
Establishes a zero-volt potential reference, ensuring that logic HIGH (+3.3V) and logic LOW (0V) correspond directly to binary 1 and 0.





2. Firmware Code-to-Concept Breakdown








Code Statement (Framework / Bare-Metal)
Week 2 Theoretical Concept
Deep-Dive Explanation




int bitVal = (num &gt;&gt; k) & 1;
Right-Shift (&gt;&gt;) & Bitwise AND (&)
This is the core formula from Week 2 lecture. Shifting num right by k moves the target bit into the Least Significant Bit (bit 0) position. Bitwise ANDing with 1 masks out all upper bits, isolating the single binary digit 0 or 1.


uint8_t num; vs int num;
Fixed-Width Types (&lt;stdint.h&gt;)
On a 32-bit ARM Cortex-M processor, a standard int occupies 4 bytes (32 bits). Since the value only ranges from 0 to 15, declaring uint8_t uses exactly 1 byte—reducing SRAM memory usage by 75%.


static const uint8_t led_pins[4]
Memory Layout: Flash ROM vs. SRAM
Declaring the pin array with const instructs the C linker to place it in non-volatile Flash ROM (.rodata section) rather than consuming precious runtime SRAM (.data/.bss).


GPIOA_BSRR_REG = (1UL &lt;&lt; pin);GPIOA_BSRR_REG = (1UL &lt;&lt; (pin + 16));
Atomic Bit Manipulation & Race Conditions
Unlike ODR |= (1 &lt;&lt; pin) which requires a 3-step read-modify-write cycle (LDR, ORR, STR), writing to BSRR sets or resets a specific pin in a single clock cycle without altering other pins, preventing interrupt corruption.

