Understanding how every circuit component and line of code connects back to our Week 1 theoretical principles is essential for mastering embedded systems engineering:

1. Hardware & Circuit-Level Concept Mapping








Circuit Component
Week 1 Theoretical Concept
Technical Function in System




ATmega328P / Nucleo MCU
Microcontroller (MCU) Architecture
Integrates CPU, Flash ROM (program code), SRAM (variables), and GPIO peripherals on a single silicon die. Unlike a general-purpose MPU (e.g., Intel Core i7), it requires no external RAM or chipset to boot.


LED (Light Emitting Diode)
Digital Actuator & Output Load
Converts digital electrical energy into photons. It serves as our physical feedback mechanism to observe binary output states (HIGH = ON, LOW = OFF).


220Ω Resistor
Pin Current Limitation & Protection
Microcontroller GPIO pins have a maximum source/sink current rating (typically 20–40 mA). Without this resistor, the LED would draw excessive current, destroying the MCU’s internal output driver MOSFETs.


GND & VCC Connections
Closed Electrical Circuit & Ground Reference
Microcontrollers rely on a unified 0V ground reference to measure voltage potentials accurately.





2. Firmware Code-to-Concept Breakdown








Code Statement (Framework / Bare-Metal)
